<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mosul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mosul is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, opposite the ruins of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>. In the twelfth century it was a base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>, in whose service <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and his family rose to power. Its politics were thus closely linked to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosul.jpg" alt="Mosul" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copyright © 2013 Younus Alhamdani · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B5%D9%84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…took Damascus that autumn, progressively absorbed the Zengid principalities of Syria and northern Mesopotamia through a combination of military pressure and diplomacy, sent his brother Turanshah to conquer Yemen in 1174, securing the Red Sea route, took Aleppo in 1183 and received the submission of <b>Mosul</b> in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the Hamdanids of <b>Mosul</b> in the north, the Samanids of Khurasan and the Ziyarids of Gurgan in the east, and rival dynasties in Kerman and Oman, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling family descended from the Baharlu of the Yıwa tribe of the Oghuz Turks, who by the fourteenth century held lands north of Lake Van and around <b>Mosul</b> in Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1127, after the murder of Aqsunqur al-Bursuqi, atabeg of <b>Mosul</b>, the Seljuks named Zengi, son of Aq Sunqur al-Hajib, their former governor of Aleppo, as the new atabeg; he had already been a successful Seljuk general and governor in Iraq.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the 12th century Yazidis appear in the sources as Dāsinī, after the region of Dāsin northeast of <b>Mosul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Heavily outnumbered, Saladin took the hills by the Orontes and surrounded the attacking armies of <b>Mosul</b> and Aleppo.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
